{
  "_provenance": {
    "kind": "curated",
    "description": "Vanilla RangedAttribute default/min/max for every attribute name that appears in any protocol's minecraft:attribute registry. KEYED BY RAW DATASET NAME, not by the prefix-stripped canonical name, because minecraft:horse.jump_strength (735-765) and minecraft:generic.jump_strength (766-767) canonicalise to the same string and carry DIFFERENT defaults AND ranges (0.7 in [0,2] versus 0.42 in [0,32]). AttributeInstance consumes both numbers - it seeds BaseValue from the default and clamps into [min,max] - so one shared row would be silently wrong for one era. Canonicalisation happens only at LOOKUP time, in Umpk.Game.Entities.AttributeIds.",
    "sources": [
      "MinecraftOfficial/1.17-decompiled/net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/attributes/Attributes.java (horse.jump_strength, the 735-765 era)",
      "MinecraftOfficial/1.20.6-decompiled/net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/attributes/Attributes.java (the 766 set; values identical to 1.21.1 for every shared name)",
      "MinecraftOfficial/1.21.1-decompiled/net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/attributes/Attributes.java (31 of the 40 canonical names)",
      "MinecraftOfficial/1.21.11-decompiled/net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/attributes/Attributes.java (camera_distance, tempt_range, waypoint_transmit_range, waypoint_receive_range)",
      "MinecraftOfficial/26.2-decompiled/net/minecraft/world/entity/ai/attributes/Attributes.java (air_drag_modifier, below_name_distance, bounciness, friction_modifier, name_tag_distance)"
    ],
    "vanilla_cite_format": "<decompile tree>:<line in that tree's Attributes.java>",
    "float_literal_note": "Three vanilla defaults are FLOAT literals widened to double by RangedAttribute's double constructor: movement_speed 0.7F (1.17:14, 1.20.6:61; a plain 0.7 double from 1.21.1:75, 1.21.11:68, 26.2:78), flying_speed 0.4F (1.17:17, 1.20.6:37; a plain 0.4 from 1.21.1:45, 1.21.11:42, 26.2:49), and jump_strength 0.42F on every era that has it (1.20.6:46, 1.21.1:54, 1.21.11:49, 26.2:59). This table stores the decimal, which is exact for the modern literals and differs from the widened float by under 1.2e-8 for the legacy ones. No single raw row could be exact for both anyway: minecraft:generic.movement_speed spans 735-767, across which vanilla itself changed the literal. The difference is unobservable: a registry default is only an AttributeInstance's PRE-FIRST-PACKET base, every update_attributes carries base and overwrites it, and the local player's own seeds come from Player.createAttributes (movement_speed 0.1), never from the registry default.",
    "protocols_covered": [
      735,
      736,
      751,
      753,
      754,
      755,
      756,
